sting, I can use generic Mockito stubs with any() for RowMapper and ArgumentCaptor to capture and verify the RowMapper behavior, which works fine since each test focuses on one method at a time.\n\nI'll capture the RowMapper using ArgumentCaptor, then invoke it directly on a mock ResultSet to verify the mapping logic works correctly for methods like getAlleKunden. I should set up the ResultSet mock with lenient stubbing and check that the resulting map contains the expected values, keeping in mind that DATUM uses a static SimpleDateFormat.\n\nFor neuerAuftrag, I need to mock multiple jdbcTemplate calls in sequence: first querying for the max order number, then inserting the new order and getting back its ID, optionally updating if kmStand exists, and finally calling getAuftrag internally which triggers more query calls for the Auftrag and its Positionen. Since everything goes through the mocked jdbcTemplate, I'll need to stub each of these calls in the right order.\n\nThe tricky part is that both the main getAuftrag query and the getPositionen call use the same jdbcTemplate.query signature with a single long parameter, so I can't easily differentiate them by argument count alone—I'll need to use matchers like anyString() for the SQL and any(RowMapper.class) to handle the different row mappers each call uses. Setting up generic stubs for the chain calls using argument matchers that check SQL content - one for queries returning empty position lists and another for auftrag queries that return a populated object with the right id and status. The tricky part is that the internal RowMapper implementations need to match what the actual code expects when it processes the ResultSet. For the mapping tests, I'll capture the RowMapper and manually invoke mapRow with a mocked ResultSet to verify each field gets mapped correctly across all the domain objects. Then I need to set up the Mockito matchers carefully for the jdbcTemplate.query call—using anyString() for the SQL, any(RowMapper.class) for the mapper, and any(Object[].class) to handle the varargs arguments flexibly. For varargs matching in Mockito, I can use `any(Object[].class)` to match the entire array as a single matcher, which handles the autoboxing cleanly. Since different test scenarios pass varying numbers of varargs arguments, I should either create specific stubs for each case with matchers that match the actual argument count, or use `argThat` with a custom matcher on the SQL content to keep things flexible and avoid the complexity of varargs matching altogether.\n\nRather than overthinking the matcher strategy, I'll take a pragmatic approach: handle each test case individually with straightforward stubbing, use `ArgumentCaptor` to verify calls when needed, and keep return values tightly controlled for the specific invocation in that test. Since each test typically triggers only one relevant call context, I can use distinct matchers to prevent ambiguity when multiple stubs are applied to the same mock. The goal is reasonably thorough coverage focusing on CRUD methods—testing that the right arguments are passed and that the mapping logic works correctly—without getting bogged down in perfect matcher elegance.\n\nNow I'm planning out the test cases for the remaining methods: status transitions with happy and error paths, mocking `getAuftrag` by stubbing the query calls to return canned `Auftrag` objects, then covering `neuePosition`, `erstelleRechnung`, `neuerAuftrag`, and the admin methods like `statistik`, `bereinigeStornierte`, `getMonatsBericht`, and `getTopKunden`. For methods like `getAuftrag` that make multiple internal queries, I'll stub both `jdbcTemplate.query` overloads using matchers that inspect the SQL content. I'm also thinking about creating a helper method to mock the `getAuftrag` behavior since it's a public method that gets called internally by other methods.\n\nWriting test helper method... The issue is that JdbcTemplate has two query overloads: one taking just the SQL and RowMapper, and another with varargs for additional parameters. When I call `query(sql, mapper, id)`, Java resolves to the varargs version and wraps `id` into an Object array, so my mock using `any(Object[].class)` should correctly match that call. For varargs matching in Mockito, using `any(Object[].class)` works when the method is invoked with individual arguments since Mockito automatically wraps them into an array. Rather than overthinking the matcher specifics, I'll simplify by using lenient mode and generic `any()` matchers across the board to handle the varying argument counts across different invocations. the compiler resolves the overload at compile time based on the exact method signature I'm calling, so for queries with a single vararg parameter like `anyLong()`, autoboxing converts the primitive to a Long object that gets wrapped into the varargs array, and this approach should work across all the different query methods.\n\nWhen multiple queries within the same test share the same overload shape—like getAuftrag and getPositionen both taking a single long argument—I need to differentiate them using `argThat` matchers on the SQL string to check for specific keywords, combined with the long matcher, so each stubbed call targets only its intended query.\n\nFor getAuftraege(status), the parameter is already declared as Object[], so when passed to the varargs method it's sent directly as the array itself rather than wrapped further. I can match this with `any(Object[].class)` for the varargs portion, but I need to be careful about conflicts with the single-argument matchers used elsewhere.\n\nActually, looking at both branches—when mitStatus is true, args becomes a single-element array {status}, and when false, it's an empty Object[0]—both are Object[] instances that match the same `any(Object[].class)` matcher, so a single generic stub works for both cases.\n\nLet me verify the actual calls using ArgumentCaptor to ensure correctness.\n\nI'll set up the test file with @Nested classes or keep it flat, using @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class) and @Mock for JdbcTemplate, then manually instantiate WerkstattService with the mocked template and a batch size of 20.\n\nFor the tests themselves, I'll start with a constructor test verifying the service initializes correctly with ustSatz=20, then move into getAlleKunden_mapsResultSetCorrectly where I mock the ResultSet to return specific values for id, customer details like name and contact info, and a timestamp for when the record was created, then stub the jdbcTemplate.query call to use that mocked RowMapper.\n\nThe key insight is that since jdbcTemplate is mocked, getAlleKunden() just returns whatever we stub it to return—the actual mapping logic is separate. So I need to capture the RowMapper argument using ArgumentCaptor, invoke it directly with a mock ResultSet to verify the Kunde object fields are mapped correctly, then separately test that getAlleKunden() returns the stubbed list.\n\nWriting the test class... Setting up mock ResultSet data... Adding test cases for the remaining methods...\n\nFor getAuftraege, I need to verify the SQL differs based on whether a status filter is provided, and test the RowMapper logic that builds kundeName from nachname and vorname, plus handles the kennzeichen and fahrzeugBezeichnung fields—particularly when marke or modell are null, since defaultString converts those to empty strings which then concatenate with spaces.\n\nFor getAuftrag, I'm checking the not-found case returning null, and the found case where the auftrag gets its positionen populated through a separate query call.\n\nFor neuerAuftrag, I'm stubbing the max ID query to return something like 3, then the insert query returns the new ID, and if kmStand exists I verify an update gets called before retrieving the full auftrag—the resulting auftragNr should follow the format \"A-<jahr>-0004\".\n\nFor setzeStatus, I'm testing that a not-found auftrag throws an exception, and verifying the allowed state transitions: ANGENOMMEN can go to IN_ARBEIT or STORNIERT, IN_ARBEIT can go to FERTIG or STORNIERT, FERTIG can go to ABGEHOLT or IN_\n\nARBEIT, while disallowed transitions like ANGENOMMEN to FERTIG throw exceptions. I also need to verify the SQL includes \"fertig_am\" when transitioning to FERTIG and \"abgeholt_am\" when transitioning to ABGEHOLT. For each test case, I'm stubbing getAuftrag to return an auftrag with the appropriate status, then reusing that same stub for the second getAuftrag call after the update—since the stub is stateless it returns the same value both times, which is fine for verifying the update was called and the returned object is consistent.\n\nFor neuePosition, I need to test that a not-found position throws an exception, that statuses like ABGEHOLT or STORNIERT throw exceptions, and that allowed statuses like ANGENOMMEN succeed with queryForObject called for the insert returning the id and position fields properly set. loeschePosition just needs to verify the update was called. For getAlleRechnungen and getRechnung, I'm testing the RowMapper logic that builds the kundeName field.\n\nFor erstelleRechnung, I need to check that a not-found Auftrag throws an exception, that a non-FERTIG status throws an exception, that an already-existing Rechnung throws an exception, and that the happy path correctly sums positions and computes netto, ust, and brutto values before verifying the insert queryForObject is called with those computed values and the final getRechnung returns the rechnung. setzeBezahlt should verify the update is called followed by getRechnung. For getMonatsBericht, I'm capturing the RowMapper and mocking the ResultSet to return monat, anzahl_auftraege, anzahl_rechnungen, umsatz_netto, and umsatz_brutto, then verifying the mapping sets jahr correctly from the closure variable.\n\nFor getTopKunden, I need to verify jdbcTemplate.queryForList is called with the correct SQL and jahr parameter twice, and that it returns a list matching the result. getAdminStatistik requires stubbing multiple queryForObject calls with distinct SQL patterns (using argThat to match different count strings) returning integers, then verifying the map contains the proper keys and a \"stand\" field with a formatted date string matching the expected pattern. bereinigeStornierte should stub queryForList to return a list of IDs, then verify delete calls are made for each ID in the correct order (auftrag_position then auftrag), and return the size; I also need to test the empty list case to ensure no deletes occur and it returns 0.\n\nI'm being careful about JdbcTemplate method signatures—queryForObject has overloads for both no-args and varargs versions, and I need to match the correct one for AdminStatistik's cal calls using argThat on SQL content to distinguish between multiple calls. queryForList also has two key overloads: one returning List<Map<String,Object>> with varargs, and another returning List<T> for a single column with a Class parameter. getTopKunden uses the varargs version while bereinigeStornierte uses the Class-based version.\n\nNow I'm setting up the mock stubs... ectly, so there's no ambiguity—the varargs overload wouldn't match since we're passing two specific argument types rather than a variable number of Objects.\n\nFor the mock setup with `getTopKunden`, I need to handle the type matching carefully since `queryForList` expects `Object...` varargs but I'm passing integers. Using `eq((Object) jahr)` or `anyInt()` should work to properly match the method signature.\n\nActually, I think the cleaner approach is just using `any()` twice for the Object arguments — Mockito's generic `any()` will infer the type as Object from context, which matches the varargs parameter type perfectly.\n\nNow moving on to the admin statistik mock setup with multiple `queryForObject` calls. Since they all use the same overload signature (String, Class<T>), I need to differentiate them by the actual SQL content. I can use `argThat` with a lambda to check for specific substrings or exact matches in the SQL — like checking if the query contains \"FROM kunde\" versus matching the exact COUNT query for fahrzeug. The SQL strings are distinct enough that substring matching should work without false positives. Mockito checks stubbed answers in reverse order, so the most recently added stub takes precedence when multiple stubs could match an invocation. This means I need to order my stubs from most general to most specific, placing the specific queries last so they override the general ones.\n\nThe issue is that using `contains(\"FROM auftrag\")` for the admin statistics query will also match the exact query since it contains that substring, creating ambiguity. Instead, I should use exact string matching with `eq()` for all the SQL queries, copying the exact SQL literals directly from the source code to avoid any overlap or ordering issues.\n\nNow I'm writing out the complete test file, being careful with imports for ResultSet, Timestamp, and the sql.Date type for the Fahrzeug pickerl date handling.\n\nFor the getAuftraege RowMapper test, I'll capture the RowMapper using ArgumentCaptor when the service calls jdbcTemplate.query, then invoke it with a mock ResultSet containing the necessary fields like id, auftrag_nr, and others.\n\nI need to handle the km_stand field carefully since it uses the rs.wasNull() pattern—when km_stand is present, I'll mock getInt to return the value and wasNull to return false, but when testing the null case, I'll have getInt return 0 with wasNull returning true so kmStand gets set to null.\n\nSince mapFahrzeug has two int fields (baujahr and km_stand), both followed by wasNull() checks, I need to be aware that stubbing wasNull() globally means both fields get the same nullness treatment. That's actually fine—I can test one scenario where both are non-null, then separately test where both are null.\n\nFor Auftrag, there's just the one km_stand field, so I'll test both wasNull() true and false cases there too. Now I'm moving on to Rechnung mapping, which has more fields to handle: id, rechnung_nr, auftrag_id, ausgestellt_am (timestamp), summe_netto (double), ust (double), summe_brutto (double), bezahlt (boolean), bezahlt_am (timestamp), plus auftrag_nr and n in the wrapper methods.\n\nLet me verify the Auftrag class has setKundeId, which it does. For AuftragPosition, I need to map id, auftragId, typ, bezeichnung, menge (double), einzelpreis (double), and there's a getGesamtpreis() method but it's not directly needed for the service tests. The RowMapper for getPositionen is straightforward. For MonatsBericht, the RowMapper pulls jahr from the closure variable passed as a method parameter, then maps mon...\n\nWriting test code... I need to be careful about JdbcTemplate's overloaded query methods—the 2-arg version without varargs is what the source code uses, and that's what my mock matcher targets, so there's no ambiguity across different service methods since each test only invokes one. Let me verify the implementation details next. (130*20)/100 = 26.0 netto, so brutto = 156.0 - those are my test values. For neuerAuftrag, I need to dynamically compute the year using Calendar to match what the code does, then build the expected auftrag number and SQL pattern accordingly. I also need to test both paths in the kmStand logic - one where it's provided and triggers a fahrzeug update, and another where it's null to verify the update doesn't happen. Writing mock verification... For the verify call, I need matchers that match the method signature—using argThat for the SQL string and any(Object[].class) for the varargs should compile and work correctly since Mockito will just find zero matches when verifying the call never happened. Rather than wrestling with matcher complexity, I'll split this into two simpler tests: one checking the case where kmStand gets set.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasonin